Where to stay in Amboise? It all depends on what you are coming to do: walk around the town, fan out to the châteaux, or not move from a park for two days. We run a charming hotel ten minutes from the centre, on the other bank of the Loire, and every week we meet guests who booked in the wrong place for their stay. Here is how to choose.

## Where to stay in Amboise: town centre or countryside?

To stay in Amboise, choose the town centre if you want to do everything on foot, and the countryside or the north bank if you are looking for calm, space and a pool. Both are worth it, they just do not suit the same trip.

The historic centre sits between the royal château, the Rue Nationale and the Clos Lucé. You are as close as possible to the monuments, you can dine without taking the car, and you hear the town in the evening. The downside is well known: few parking spaces, rooms that are often smaller, and streets packed in July and August.

The north bank, in Nazelles-Négron or Pocé-sur-Cisse, is ten minutes from the centre by car. You gain silence, gardens and a view of the hillside. The countryside to the south, towards the Amboise forest and Chenonceau, suits families who want a garden and do not mind driving.

Our view is simple: for a stay of two nights or more in the valley, the nearby countryside wins. Amboise can be seen in a day, and the rest of your time is spent elsewhere.

## Which type of accommodation should you choose in Amboise?

In Amboise, accommodation is split between hotels, bed and breakfasts, holiday cottages, private rentals and the Île d'Or campsite. The right choice depends on the size of your group and the level of service you expect.

### The hotel, for service without constraints

A hotel offers a reception desk, daily housekeeping and flexible hours. It is the simplest option for a weekend. The 1 to 5 star rating is awarded by Atout France for five years, on criteria checked by an accredited body: it is the only official benchmark of service level, with online reviews coming after.

### Staying with a host, for a home-from-home welcome

A bed and breakfast means staying with someone who welcomes you in person, breakfast included. The law limits it to five rooms and fifteen people. You come for the conversation and the tips, and you accept a more family setting. If the spirit appeals but you want the service of a hotel, we explain the difference in our article on [the guesthouse spirit in a château near Amboise](/en/blog/bed-and-breakfast-amboise).

### The holiday cottage or rental, for independence

A holiday cottage or a private rental suits longer stays and families who want to cook. You often pay for cleaning separately, you do your own shopping, and nobody tells you which cellar to visit.

### The Île d'Or campsite, for a small budget

The Île d'Or campsite sits on the island, facing the royal château. It is the cheapest view in town, and a good base for anyone arriving on the Loire à Vélo.

![Reading corner in one of the château's bedrooms, window open onto the trees of the grounds, inlaid chest of drawers and an armchair with a striped cushion](/images/inline/chambres/chambre-the-01.webp "The Thé bedroom. The château's 10 bedrooms are air-conditioned and none looks like the last.")

## Where to stay in Amboise with a pool?

To stay near Amboise with a pool, look outside the historic centre: hotels in the old town almost never have room to install one. The addresses with a pool are on the hillsides and in the countryside.

It is one of the criteria our guests mention most in summer, and it really matters when it is 32 °C in July after a morning of sightseeing. Our 16-metre lap pool is heated from April to October, at the far end of the grounds, with its pool house. Always check a pool's opening dates before booking: many close at the end of August.

![The château's lap pool, a long blue basin lined with lawn and tall trees, white pool house at the far end](/images/inline/piscine/piscine-03.webp "The 16-metre lap pool, heated from April to October. It is at the end of the grounds, away from the bedrooms and the noise.")

## How many nights should you plan in Amboise?

Plan two nights in Amboise to see the town and one big neighbouring château, three or four to add the cellars, the Loire à Vélo and a day with no plans. A single night forces you to choose between the town and Chenonceau.

The first day is spent in town: the royal château at opening time, the Clos Lucé in the afternoon, the Île d'Or at sunset. The details are in our guide to [things to do in Amboise](/en/blog/things-to-do-amboise). The second day heads for Chenonceau or Chaumont-sur-Loire. Beyond that, our article on [visiting the Loire châteaux](/en/blog/visiting-loire-chateaux) gives the order we recommend.

## When should you book accommodation in Amboise?

Book your accommodation in Amboise two to three months ahead for a weekend between May and September, and as early as possible for the May bank holiday weekends. Out of season, a few days are often enough.

The Ascension and Whitsun weekends fill up first, even before July and August. By contrast, November and March are months we defend: the châteaux stay open, the queues disappear and room prices dip almost everywhere in the valley.

## Sleeping in a château near Amboise

To sleep in a château near Amboise, the Château de la Huberdière is a charming 3-star hotel with 10 bedrooms in Nazelles-Négron, ten minutes from the town centre and 2 hrs 20 from Paris. The enclosed grounds cover 14 hectares.

We are not in the centre, and we would rather say so straight away: you need a car, or a taxi from Amboise station. In return, you get the silence of a park, a large lake, bedrooms that are all different and hosts who know the valley. In the evening, from May to September, the château's table welcomes residents, and on Thursdays dinner is Italian; out of season, it opens on request for groups of six or more. It is all on our page about [château dining](/en/chateau-dining-touraine).

What we do not offer: no spa, no bike hire, no night-time room service. Our homemade breakfast is 15 € per person, served on the terrace whenever the weather allows.

For a group, the estate can be booked for exclusive use and sleeps up to 21 people. It is the option chosen by families and groups of friends who want to [rent a château with friends or family](/en/blog/rent-chateau-group-weekend) rather than book five rooms at five addresses. Our [families and groups](/en/chateau-hire-large-group) page sets out the terms.

